============================================================
THE PARTS OF MEDICARE
============================================================
Part A (Hospital insurance): Covers inpatient hospital stays, skilled nursing facility care after a hospital stay, hospice, and some home health care. Premium-free for most people (those who, or whose spouse, paid Medicare taxes for about 10 years / 40 quarters). A deductible applies for each hospital benefit period.

Part B (Medical insurance): Covers doctor visits, outpatient care, lab tests, preventive screenings, and durable medical equipment. Has a monthly premium and an annual deductible; after the deductible you generally pay 20% of approved costs. Original Medicare places no annual cap on that 20%, which is a primary reason people add Medigap or choose Medicare Advantage.

Part C (Medicare Advantage): Private plans that bundle Part A, Part B, and usually Part D into one package. Often add dental, vision, hearing, and fitness benefits, sometimes with a $0 premium. Use provider networks and set an annual out-of-pocket maximum.

Part D (Prescription drug coverage): Private plans covering prescription medications; each plan has its own formulary (drug list). In 2026 out-of-pocket drug costs are capped at $2,100 per year. A permanent late-enrollment penalty can apply if you skip it when first eligible without other creditable coverage.

Medigap (Medicare Supplement): Private policies that help pay the deductibles, copays, and coinsurance Original Medicare leaves to you. Standardized by letter (A, B, D, G, K, L, M, N; Plans C and F are closed to people newly eligible after January 1, 2020). The same letter means the same coverage regardless of insurer; only price and service differ. Best purchased during the one-time 6-month Medigap Open Enrollment Period that begins when you are 65 and enrolled in Part B (guaranteed acceptance, no medical underwriting). Cannot be used with a Medicare Advantage plan.

============================================================
2026 MEDICARE COSTS (Source: CMS)
============================================================
Part A:
- Monthly premium: $0 for those with 40+ work quarters; $311/month with 30–39 quarters; $565/month with fewer than 30 quarters.
- Hospital deductible: $1,736 per benefit period.
- Hospital days 1–60: $0/day (after deductible). Days 61–90: $434/day. Lifetime reserve days (91–150): $868/day (60 days over a lifetime). After day 150: all costs.
- Skilled nursing facility: $0/day days 1–20; $217/day days 21–100; all costs after day 100.
- A benefit period begins on admission and ends after 60 consecutive days out of a hospital or skilled nursing facility; more than one can occur in a year.

Part B:
- Standard monthly premium: $202.90. Annual deductible: $283. You pay 20% after the deductible, with no annual out-of-pocket cap under Original Medicare.
- Late-enrollment penalty: +10% of the premium for each full 12 months you delayed without other qualifying coverage; usually permanent.

Part C (Medicare Advantage) and Part D:
- Medicare Advantage average national premium: about $14/month (varies by plan and area).
- Part D premium: varies by plan.
- Part D out-of-pocket cap: $2,100 in 2026; once reached, covered drugs cost $0 for the rest of the year.
- Medicare Prescription Payment Plan: optional, spreads drug costs into monthly installments at no extra charge.
- Part D late-enrollment penalty: roughly 1% of the national base premium per month without creditable coverage; permanent.

IRMAA (Income-Related Monthly Adjustment Amount):
- Higher-income surcharges on Part B and Part D start above $109,000 (single) or $218,000 (married filing jointly), based on modified adjusted gross income from two years earlier (2024 income for 2026).
- Total Part B premium with IRMAA ranges roughly $284–$690/month in 2026. If income dropped due to a life event such as retirement, you can ask Social Security to reconsider.

============================================================
ENROLLMENT PERIODS AND DEADLINES
============================================================
- Initial Enrollment Period (IEP): 7 months — the 3 months before your 65th-birthday month, the birthday month, and the 3 months after. Enrolling in the first 3 months allows coverage to begin as early as the month you turn 65.
- Fall Open Enrollment / Annual Enrollment Period (AEP): October 15 – December 7 each year; anyone with Medicare can join, switch, or drop Medicare Advantage or Part D plans; changes take effect January 1.
- Medicare Advantage Open Enrollment Period: January 1 – March 31; those already in a Medicare Advantage plan may switch to another Advantage plan or return to Original Medicare and add a drug plan.
- General Enrollment Period (GEP): January 1 – March 31 for people who missed their IEP and lack a special exception; coverage starts the month after enrollment; late penalties may apply.
- Special Enrollment Period (SEP): triggered by qualifying events — losing employer coverage when you retire (generally 8 months to enroll in Part B without penalty), moving out of a plan's service area, qualifying for Extra Help, and others. COBRA generally does NOT count as coverage that protects against the Part B penalty.
- Medigap Open Enrollment Period: one-time 6-month window beginning when you are 65 and enrolled in Part B; insurers cannot deny coverage or charge more for pre-existing conditions during it.

