============================================================
FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS
============================================================
Q: When am I first eligible for Medicare?
A: Most people at age 65. You can qualify earlier after 24 months of Social Security Disability benefits, or with ALS or end-stage renal disease. The Initial Enrollment Period spans the 7 months around your 65th birthday.

Q: Do I have to take Medicare at 65 if I'm still working?
A: Not necessarily. With group coverage from an employer of 20+ employees, you can often delay Part B without penalty and enroll later via a Special Enrollment Period; many still take premium-free Part A. Rules differ for small employers, COBRA, and retiree plans.

Q: Do I get Medicare automatically?
A: If you're already receiving Social Security before 65, you're usually enrolled in Parts A and B automatically. If not, you must sign up yourself through Social Security.

Q: How do I sign up for Medicare?
A: Enroll in Parts A and B through Social Security (ssa.gov/medicare, 1-800-772-1213, or a local office). Add Part D, Medicare Advantage, or Medigap through the plan, a licensed agent, or the compare-and-enroll link.

Q: What happens if I miss my enrollment window?
A: You may face permanent late penalties and delayed coverage. Part B adds 10% per year delayed; Part D adds about 1% per month without drug coverage. The General Enrollment Period (Jan 1–Mar 31) is the usual fallback, but check for a Special Enrollment Period that may waive penalties.

Q: I'm retiring and losing my work insurance. What do I do?
A: Losing job-based coverage opens a Special Enrollment Period; you generally have 8 months to enroll in Part B without penalty. COBRA usually does not protect you from the Part B penalty.

Q: Can I change my plan after I enroll?
A: Yes. Switch Advantage or Part D plans each fall (Oct 15–Dec 7); Advantage members get an extra window Jan 1–Mar 31. Changing Medigap outside your one-time window may involve medical underwriting.

Q: Original Medicare or Medicare Advantage — which is better?
A: It depends. Original Medicare + Medigap + Part D offers nationwide provider access and predictable costs at higher premiums. Medicare Advantage offers lower premiums and extra benefits but uses networks and may require referrals/prior approvals. Consider your doctors, medications, travel, and cost preferences.

Q: What is Medigap and do I need it?
A: Medigap fills Original Medicare's gaps (deductibles and the uncapped 20% coinsurance). Worth considering with Original Medicare for protection from large bills. Plans are standardized by letter; compare price and service.

Q: Does Medicare cover dental, vision, or hearing?
A: Original Medicare generally does not cover routine dental, vision, or hearing. Many Medicare Advantage plans add these; standalone policies also exist.

Q: Does Medicare work when I travel or snowbird out of state?
A: Original Medicare works anywhere in the U.S. with providers who accept it. Medicare Advantage is usually network-based, so out-of-area care may be limited to emergencies — important for Arizona snowbirds.

Q: How much will Medicare cost me in 2026?
A: For most, Part A is free and the standard Part B premium is $202.90/month with a $283 deductible. Add a Part D plan and either a Medigap premium or a Medicare Advantage plan (often low or $0). Higher earners pay IRMAA.

Q: I have limited income — can I get help paying?
A: Yes. The Medicare Savings Program can pay your Part B premium; Extra Help lowers drug costs; very limited income may qualify for AHCCCS (Arizona Medicaid). A SHIP counselor (1-800-432-4040) can screen you.

Q: What is the $2,100 drug cap?
A: In 2026, once your out-of-pocket spending on covered prescriptions reaches $2,100, you pay $0 for covered drugs the rest of the year. The Medicare Prescription Payment Plan can spread costs monthly.

Q: How do I avoid Medicare scams?
A: Medicare never calls, texts, or visits unsolicited to sell plans or request your Medicare/bank details. Protect your Medicare number, review statements, and report suspected fraud to the Senior Medicare Patrol at 1-800-432-4040.

