============================================================
GUIDE SUMMARIES
============================================================
Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap in Arizona (https://www.medicareenrollmentarizona.com/medicare-advantage-vs-medigap-arizona/):
Medicare Advantage (Part C) bundles coverage with low/$0 premiums, extra benefits (dental/vision/hearing), local networks, and a yearly out-of-pocket cap. Medigap (Medicare Supplement) pairs with Original Medicare for nationwide provider access and predictable costs at a higher premium, with a separate Part D drug plan. Snowbirds and frequent travelers often prefer Medigap for portability; those who stay local and want low premiums often prefer Advantage. Medigap is easiest to buy during the one-time 6-month Medigap Open Enrollment Period; Arizona has no continuous guaranteed-issue rule, so timing matters.

Turning 65 in Arizona: Medicare Checklist (https://www.medicareenrollmentarizona.com/turning-65-checklist-arizona/):
Start 3-6 months before turning 65. Sign up for Parts A and B through Social Security if not auto-enrolled; enrolling in the first 3 months of the IEP lets coverage begin the month you turn 65. Compare Part D, Medicare Advantage and Medigap; the birthday month also starts the one-time 6-month Medigap Open Enrollment. Review coverage every fall during Open Enrollment (Oct 15-Dec 7). Check eligibility for the Medicare Savings Program and Extra Help; if still working with employer coverage, confirm whether to delay Part B.
